  <h2>The same sentence, the same mug, every afternoon</h2>
  <p>At 2 PM, I would lose the thread of whatever I was doing.</p>
  <p>The same sentence sat on my screen. The same email stayed half-written. The same task that looked easy in the morning suddenly needed a second coffee before I could finish it.</p>
  <p>So I made another cup.</p>
  <p>That worked for a little while.</p>
  <p>Then the lift faded.</p>
  <p>The edge stayed.</p>
  <p>If the coffee made me jittery, I told myself that was normal. If I felt tired after lunch, I blamed my discipline. If I lost focus again, I reached for another drink.</p>
  <p>Coffee was supposed to fix the afternoon.</p>
  <p>Instead, it kept me inside the same loop:</p>
  <div class="loop">Lift. Fade. Reach for more. Repeat.</div>

  <h2>The cup fixed the moment. It kept the loop alive.</h2>
  <p>Coffee gives you a sharp lift. Then the lift fades.</p>
  <p>When it fades, another cup looks like the obvious answer. The next dose starts the pattern again.</p>
  <p>A shorter lift. Another reach. More edge left behind.</p>

